articles=[
{'area':'finance','slug':'are-gsecs-really-risk-free','tag':'Case studies','type':'Case study','title':'Are G-Secs Really Risk-Free?','excerpt':'A classroom question, Zimbabwe’s hyperinflation, and the difference between getting your money back and keeping its value.','time':'4 min read','body':(ROOT/'content'/'gsecs.html').read_text()},
{'area':'finance','slug':'the-financial-problem-first','tag':'Financial thinking','type':'Field note','title':'The financial problem comes first. The model can wait.','excerpt':'The questions I want answered before I get carried away with a very impressive notebook.','time':'3 min read','body':'''<p class="article-lead">I like building things. Which makes “let me code it” a very convenient way to avoid admitting I haven’t completely understood the problem yet.</p><p>Finance makes that habit especially interesting. There is a model to learn, a dataset to explore, and usually an acronym nearby to make the whole thing feel official. I want the financial intuition underneath all of that.</p><h2>Start with the decision</h2><p>The question I’m learning to ask first is: whose decision is this supposed to improve? A researcher looking for a signal, a team assessing risk, and someone reviewing a suspicious transaction are doing different jobs. “Apply AI to finance” is a field of interest. It still needs an actual problem.</p><p>For any idea, I want to be able to explain the financial workflow, the information available, the decision being made, and the cost of getting it wrong. Preferably in sentences that don’t require a second glossary.</p><h2>Then earn the complexity</h2><p>My learning map moves from financial products and probability towards derivatives, risk, and quantitative research. The purpose is to understand why a method belongs in a problem, what its assumptions buy me, and where those assumptions become inconvenient.</p><p>A useful small build should leave me with more than a chart. I want a question, a reproducible method, and an explanation of what I still don’t know. The limitations deserve their own paragraph. They have usually worked quite hard to be there.</p><h2>The direction I’m taking</h2><p>I want to stay close to engineering while developing the financial depth to build more useful systems. Eventually, I want to connect the problem, the mathematics, the implementation, and the business judgment. For now, the next step is a smaller question answered properly.</p>'''},
{'area':'finance','slug':'when-financial-ai-starts-acting','tag':'Financial AI','type':'Open question','title':'When financial AI starts acting, who owns the decision?','excerpt':'Prediction is interesting. Permission, risk limits, and accountability are where my questions begin.','time':'3 min read','body':'''<p class="article-lead">An agent that can act on a financial decision is an interesting idea. “It seemed confident” is a less interesting explanation afterwards.</p><p>One question running through my fintech exploration is how systems might move from prediction to recommendation and, in some settings, execution. I’m treating that as a direction to investigate, not a claim that every financial decision should be automated.</p><h2>What exactly is being delegated?</h2><p>Summarising a document, flagging a change, proposing an action, and taking that action are different responsibilities. Before thinking about an agent’s architecture, I want to understand its permission boundary. What can it see? What can it change? When does it have to stop and ask a person?</p><h2>The less glamorous questions</h2><p>I’m interested in the data trail behind a conclusion, the limits on an action, and the person who remains accountable. How would I investigate a bad outcome? How would the system behave when the situation no longer resembles the examples it was built around?</p><p>These questions make the project harder. That is probably a useful sign. A polished demo can skip the awkward conversation; a real financial workflow eventually has it.</p><h2>A place to begin</h2><p>A research assistant that connects a claim to its sources feels like a useful starting experiment. I’d want to observe where it helps, where it confidently fills a gap, and what a human needs to verify. I’m more interested in learning that boundary than in giving the system a bigger job title.</p><p>The technical work and the financial judgment belong together. That is the part I want to understand deeply.</p>'''},
{'area':'engineering','slug':'a-dataset-is-not-a-finished-product','tag':'Data engineering','type':'Field note','title':'A dataset is not a finished product. It is an opening statement.','excerpt':'The questions I care about after “the pipeline ran successfully.”','time':'2 min read','body':'''<p class="article-lead">The pipeline finished. Excellent. Now comes the small matter of whether someone can use what came out of it.</p><p>The engineering questions that interest me extend beyond moving data from one place to another. I want to know what the records mean, where they came from, and whether the next person can tell when something has gone wrong.</p><h2>Make the next person’s job possible</h2><p>For a dataset I would hand to someone else, I’d want a clear owner, an explanation of its fields, a trace back to its source, and a statement of its freshness and known limitations. A table named <code>final_v3_really_final</code> communicates a journey. It does not communicate a contract.</p><p>I find the idea of treating data as a product useful here: discoverability, understandable semantics, and trustworthiness are part of what makes data usable. Zhamak Dehghani’s writing on data mesh is a good starting point for that perspective.</p><h2>My question before the next layer</h2><p>If I build a model or a financial research tool on top of this dataset, what am I assuming about it? Can I check those assumptions? Would I notice a change? Those are the questions I want my engineering decisions to make easier to answer.</p><p>Moving the data is work. Making it understandable is also work. I’d like both parts to appear in the definition of done.</p><div class="reading-source">Further reading: <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/data-monolith-to-mesh.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Zhamak Dehghani — data as a product ↗</a></div>'''},
{'area':'engineering','slug':'learning-with-something-to-show','tag':'Computer science','type':'Working method','title':'A learning plan should eventually produce something other than a learning plan.','excerpt':'Notes, small builds, and the uncomfortable usefulness of explaining something without looking it up.','time':'2 min read','body':'''<p class="article-lead">I enjoy a well-organised roadmap. Possibly enough to need a roadmap for when to stop organising it.</p><p>My own approach is to connect learning to something I can explain, reproduce, or build. Reading gives me a first pass. Trying to use the idea tells me where that first pass was a little optimistic.</p><h2>A smaller definition of progress</h2><p>For a concept, I want a short explanation in my own words and a worked example I can reproduce. For a system, I want to understand the inputs, the failure modes, and the decisions behind the design. For a project, I want a functioning artifact and a readable explanation of its limits.</p><h2>Stay with the difficult sentence</h2><p>“I understand it, I just can’t explain it” is a sentence I’m trying to become more suspicious of. Sometimes the vocabulary is missing. Sometimes the understanding is. Either way, writing it down gives me something specific to work on.</p><p>This section is where I’ll work through computer science, data engineering, and the systems behind financial applications. Some notes will be small. I would rather a small idea be clear than a large one be decorated.</p><h2>Keep the loop short</h2><p>Learn a thing. Try a thing. Write down what broke. Revisit the explanation. That is the loop I want to get better at. The beautifully formatted tracker is allowed to watch.</p>'''}]

for area,label,headline,desc in [('finance','02 / FINANCE','Follow the money.<br><em>Question the assumptions.</em>','Notes on markets, financial models, and the decisions behind them. I like a compelling story. I also want to see what is holding it up.'),('engineering','03 / COMPUTER SCIENCE & DATA ENGINEERING','Under the hood.<br><em>Past “it works.”</em>','Systems, data, and the details that become very important the moment you ignore them. Notes from what I know and what I’m working to understand.')]:
    items=[a for a in articles if a['area']==area]
    filters='<div class="filter-bar" aria-label="Filter notes"><div class="filter-options">'+''.join(f'<button class="filter {"active" if i==0 else ""}" data-filter="{x}" aria-pressed="{"true" if i==0 else "false"}">{x}</button>' for i,x in enumerate(['All notes']+[a['tag'] for a in items]))+'</div><label class="search"><span class="sr-only">Search notes</span><input type="search" placeholder="Search notes" aria-label="Search notes"><span aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></label></div>'
    cards=''.join(f'<article class="note-card" data-category="{a["tag"]}"><div class="note-number">0{i+1}</div><div><p class="eyebrow">{a["tag"].upper()} <span> / </span> {a["type"].upper()}</p><h2><a href="/{area}/{a["slug"]}/">{a["title"]}</a></h2><p>{a["excerpt"]}</p><span class="read-time">{a["time"]}</span></div><a class="round-arrow" href="/{area}/{a["slug"]}/" aria-label="Read: {html.escape(a["title"])}">↗</a></article>' for i,a in enumerate(items))
    blog_heading='<section id="blogs" class="finance-blogs-heading"><p class="eyebrow">THE FINANCE JOURNAL</p><h2>Blogs</h2><p>Case studies, field notes, and questions worth staying with.</p></section>' if area=='finance' else ''
    page=intro(label,headline,desc)+blog_heading+filters+'<section class="notes-list" aria-label="Notes">'+cards+'</section><p id="no-results" class="no-results" hidden>No notes match that search. Try another word.</p>'
    write(area,layout(area,'Finance' if area=='finance' else 'CS & Data Engineering',page))
    for a in items:
        body=f'<a class="back-link" href="/{area}/">← All {"finance" if area=="finance" else "engineering"} notes</a><header class="article-header"><p class="eyebrow">{a["tag"].upper()} / {a["type"].upper()}</p><h1>{a["title"]}</h1><p>Nandini Thakur <span>·</span> {a["time"]}</p></header><article class="prose">{a["body"]}</article><div class="article-end">Still thinking this through. A good counterargument is welcome.</div>'
        write(area+'/'+a['slug'],layout(area,a['title'],body,a['excerpt'],True))

ideas=intro('04 / IDEAS & EXPLORATION','Some “what ifs”<br><em>deserve a prototype.</em>','Business questions, financial technology, and ideas I’m taking apart to see whether there is anything worth building inside.')+'''<p class="section-note"><span class="status-dot"></span> Working hypotheses. Open to a good counterargument.</p><section class="idea-list"><details open><summary><span class="idea-index">01</span><div><p class="eyebrow">FINANCIAL DATA / RESEARCH</p><h2>Connect the company.<br>Then question the signal.</h2></div><span class="plus">+</span></summary><div class="idea-body"><p>Can connecting issuers across identifiers and news create a more useful starting point for financial research?</p><div class="idea-columns"><div><h3>The experiment</h3><p>A financial entity intelligence and event-signal pipeline using accessible public data. Start with identity, provenance, and a small reproducible research question.</p></div><div><h3>What has to earn its place</h3><p>The quality of the links, whether the signal survives sensible testing, and whether the result helps someone make a better-informed decision.</p></div></div><span class="tag">EXPLORATION · NOT A RELEASED PROJECT</span></div></details><details><summary><span class="idea-index">02</span><div><p class="eyebrow">FINANCIAL AI / PRODUCT THINKING</p><h2>A research copilot that<br>can show its working.</h2></div><span class="plus">+</span></summary><div class="idea-body"><p>What would make an AI research assistant worth trusting beyond its ability to sound helpful?</p><div class="idea-columns"><div><h3>The experiment</h3><p>A narrow research workflow that links claims back to documents, flags uncertainty, and gives the person reviewing it a clear place to intervene.</p></div><div><h3>The business question</h3><p>Which part of the workflow improves? Does it reduce effort without quietly increasing verification work or moving the risk somewhere harder to see?</p></div></div><span class="tag">WORKING HYPOTHESIS</span></div></details><details><summary><span class="idea-index">03</span><div><p class="eyebrow">BUSINESS / LEARNING SYSTEMS</p><h2>From an ambitious roadmap<br>to Tuesday’s actual work.</h2></div><span class="plus">+</span></summary><div class="idea-body"><p>A plan has to survive an ordinary week to be useful. What would make a learning system help someone follow through?</p><div class="idea-columns"><div><h3>The experiment</h3><p>A focused Notion system that connects a direction, a few active actions, evidence of learning, and a weekly review.</p></div><div><h3>The product question</h3><p>Can someone adapt it quickly and keep using it after the initial burst of enthusiasm? The template should have a job beyond looking organised.</p></div></div><a class="text-link" href="/products/">Explore the digital products ↗</a></div></details></section>'''
